{"body":"## 1. Motivation\n\nRadicle Artifact COBs store **location hints**: URLs telling a consumer where to fetch artifact bytes. HTTPS mirrors fit cleanly. Peer-to-peer fetches over `iroh-blobs` do not: we currently use an `iroh://<endpoint-id>` scheme that we invented, never specified, and don't own. Because these URLs are signed into the COB, today's shortcuts persist in every artifact's history forever.\n\nGoals:\n\n- A URL scheme we either own or that is governed by an open standard.\n- A well-defined, efficient encoding.\n- Clean separation between:\n  - _What_ (the CID)\n  - _Who_ (the [Endpoint ID](https://docs.iroh.computer/concepts/endpoints))\n  - _How to find them_ ([discovery](https://docs.iroh.computer/concepts/discovery)).\n- A spec we can point at and grow into a RIP later.\n\n## 2. What we ship today\n\nToday, the bare URL is:\n\n```\niroh://\n```\n\nwhich means \"derive the endpoint id from the radicle key that signed the COB.\" Open patch [`c884af8`](https://radicle.network/nodes/iris.radicle.xyz/rad%3Az4VYyJ9KuwMNkXGQnmKuGPGKw3inv/patches/c884af86bfb218bd0cc98a2beadc8948f487a9f1) decouples the two keys so a user can seed from multiple processes (CLI + desktop), giving URLs like:\n\n```\niroh://3291b50872415e5768dbf45e9535b6d696e759ae5bc96d1ab04e36ec741995e3\n```\n\nThree problems with this:\n\n1. **`iroh:` is unspecified and unowned.** Not IANA-registered, not claimed by the iroh project. If upstream ever mints their own meaning, our signed locations become ambiguous.\n2. **No spec.** Our use of `iroh://` is documented only in source comments; a second implementation has nothing to interoperate against.\n3. **No room for discovery.** Reaching an iroh endpoint requires a discovery service (today, radworks DNS, hardcoded in `src/share/endpoint.rs`). The current URL says nothing about how to resolve, leaving no path to federated or alternative discovery.\n\n## 3. Design considerations\n\n### 3.1 Identity vs. location\n\nThe URI should name the endpoint identity — durably, canonically — and nothing more. Network coordinates (IPs, relays) belong to discovery, which is consumer-side configuration analogous to DNS: defaults work for most cases, but there must be a path for federation.\n\nThis mirrors HTTPS: we write `https://example.com`, not `https://example.com?resolver=8.8.8.8`. Any compliant resolver works.\n\n### 3.2 Discovery information must not live in the COB schema\n\nTempting alternative: add a `discovery` field to the location action so every signed location carries its resolver. Don't do this. Discovery is per-ecosystem infrastructure, not per-artifact metadata; embedding it would sign \"uses radworks DNS\" into every artifact forever and turn operational changes (resolver rotation) into signed-COB migrations.\n\nDiscovery defaults belong in consumer config. URLs _may_ carry an optional `?via=` hint for cold-start cases where defaults won't reach the endpoint.\n\n### 3.3 Encoding\n\nIroh supports three string forms for an endpoint id:\n\n- 64-char lowercase **hex** — what `EndpointId::Display` emits and what the iroh team has confirmed is the [canonical string form](https://github.com/n0-computer/iroh/discussions/4264#discussioncomment-16929044).\n- 52-char standard **base32** (RFC 4648, no padding) — accepted by `FromStr`, not emitted by default.\n- 52-char **z-base-32** — `to_z32()`/`from_z32()`; the encoding pkarr uses for endpoint DNS subdomains, including radworks's `dns.radworks.xyz`.\n\nThe argument for hex is upstream alignment: it's the canonical form, so deferring to it minimizes divergence. The argument for either base32 is ~19% denser strings; z-base-32 specifically also aligns with what discovery already publishes, so a user might see the same string in a URL and in a DNS record.\n\nFor comparability with the rest of the Radicle ecosystem, we also briefly considered the `did:key:` multibase+multicodec encoding (the `z6Mk...` form Radicle DIDs use). See §4.2.\n\nOpen: hex (canonical), standard base32 (denser, well-known), or z-base-32 (denser, stack-aligned with discovery). Listed in §5 for reviewers.\n\n## 4. Candidate schemes\n\n### 4.1 Keep `iroh://`, just tighten encoding\n\nSwitch to base32, write a spec, move on. **Rejected:** doesn't address scheme ownership.\n\n### 4.2 `did:key:`\n\nW3C's `did:key:` method; same encoding as Radicle DIDs (`did:key:z6Mk...`).\n\n**Pros:** zero name-squatting (`did:` is IANA-registered, `did:key:` is W3C-specified); visually consistent with NIDs; multibase + multicodec self-describes the key type and accommodates future curves.\n\n**Cons:** says nothing about transport — readers must know by convention that \"did:key: here means iroh-blobs\"; semantic mismatch (DIDs identify _subjects_, not transport endpoints); visually indistinguishable from NIDs in the same COB; `?via=` discovery hints conflict with DID Core's reserved use of query strings.\n\n### 4.3 Mint `radiroh:` (Radicle-namespaced sibling)\n\nPlain ASCII alphabetic scheme; endpoint id encoded in the URL host (encoding per §3.3); optional `?via=` hint.\n\n**Pros:** Radicle owns the namespace; self-describes transport; query parameters are native URI machinery, so `?via=` (and future hints) grow cleanly; spec we write once and own.\n\n**Cons:** custom schemes have weaker tooling support (markdown/chat autolinking often misses them); one more thing for the ecosystem; future transports become parallel schemes (`radipfs:`, …) rather than one unified namespace.\n\n### 4.4 Extend RIP 4's `rad:` scheme\n\nPush iroh hints into the existing `rad:` URI via query params or authority sub-form.\n\n**Pros:** one canonical Radicle scheme, one `web+rad:` registration.\n\n**Cons:** iroh endpoint ids are neither NIDs nor resources-inside-a-repo, so fitting them into RIP 4's grammar muddies it. RIP coordination overhead. Couples our wire format to a scheme designed for different purposes.\n\n### 4.5 Discussion\n\n§4.1 and §4.4 are weak: the first doesn't address scheme ownership, the second commits us to RIP coordination for a relatively narrow concern. The real choice is between §4.2 (`did:key:`) and §4.3 (`radiroh:`), and it is a genuine trade-off rather than a clear win:\n\n- `did:key:` defers entirely to existing standards and stays visually consistent with Radicle DIDs, at the cost of saying nothing about transport and forcing any future discovery hint to overload DID Core query-string semantics.\n- `radiroh:` lets us own the spec and grow cleanly into discovery hints and future transports, at the cost of minting a new scheme that no tooling recognizes by default.\n\nThe deciding axis is how heavily we weight *future-proofing discovery and additional transports* (favors `radiroh:`) versus *minimizing novel surface area* (favors `did:key:`). Both are defensible; we leave the call to reviewers (see §7).\n\nRegardless of which scheme is chosen, the encoding question (§3.3) is independent, and existing `iroh://<hex>` URLs stay accepted on read for back-compat.\n\n## 5. Open questions for reviewers\n\n1. **Scheme: `did:key:` (§4.2) vs `radiroh:` (§4.3)?** The central choice. Trade-off summarized in §4.5.\n2. **Encoding (§3.3): hex (canonical), standard base32 (denser), or z-base-32 (denser + stack-aligned with discovery)?**\n3. If `radiroh:`, is the name right, or do we prefer something transport-neutral like `radseed:` to leave room for future P2P transports under one scheme?\n4. Should the `?via=` discovery-hint mechanism (§3.2) be specified up front or deferred to a follow-up once a second discovery service exists?\n5. Land the eventual spec as `docs/uri-scheme.md` first, or start as a RIP?\n6. Sunset clause for legacy `iroh:` reads? (My instinct: no — signed COBs are forever, reading is cheap.)\n\nA draft specification will follow once §1 and §2 are settled.\n","type":"comment"}